  "meta_story": {
    "id": "computing_pioneers",
    "title": "Architects of Computation",
    "tagline": "From engines to ethics",
    "description": "“Computing pioneers” are not one tribe but a relay team: theorists who defined what it means to compute, engineers who made machines obey, software innovators who taught hardware to speak human intentions, and public-facing builders who carried computation from laboratories into everyday life.\n\nThis meta-story follows that relay across nearly two centuries. It begins with a conceptual leap—treating calculation as a programmable process—then accelerates through wartime urgency and early digital architectures, before turning to the quieter revolutions of programming methodology, compiler optimization, and parallelism. Finally, it widens into questions of power: who controls computing as it scales, and who warns about the temptations of treating machines as moral substitutes.\n\nTaken together, these figures show computing as a layered invention: ideas become architectures, architectures become software ecosystems, and ecosystems become social forces that demand criticism as much as celebration.",
